WEBVTT

NOTE Generated with faster-whisper base.en; source=videos/Workshop/Diabetic/5.1 Visual Acuity Near + Distance_220p.mp4; sourceLang=en; task=transcribe

1
00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:08.847
This video demonstrates how to perform a distance and near-visual acuity test.

2
00:00:08.847 --> 00:00:11.180
Wash your hands and don't PPE.

3
00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:17.366
You will need the arclight charts and a safety pin to make pinholes.

4
00:00:17.366 --> 00:00:20.909
The room must be quiet and at least three meters wide.

5
00:00:20.909 --> 00:00:23.807
It should be well lit, ideally with natural light.

6
00:00:23.807 --> 00:00:28.960
If you're outside, make sure the sun is on the patient's back, shining onto the chart.

7
00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:32.180
The patient should be seated comfortably, although standing is okay.

8
00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:36.479
Show the patient the chart at arm's length, ask them to point in the

9
00:00:36.479 --> 00:00:37.400
direction at the

10
00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:38.216
ease point.

11
00:00:38.216 --> 00:00:42.296
Check the patient understands and you're both happy to start.

12
00:00:42.296 --> 00:00:43.520
If the patient

13
00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:47.645
normally wears glasses for distance, use them for the test.

14
00:00:47.645 --> 00:00:49.707
If not, then test unaided.

15
00:00:49.707 --> 00:00:50.120
They

16
00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:54.020
should cover one eye with a pan with their hand, not their fingers.

17
00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:55.220
Place the chart at

18
00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:59.640
the same level as the patient's eyes at a distance of 3 metres.

19
00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.000
Start with the largest

20
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.680
letters at the top of the chart working downwards.

21
00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:06.360
Stop the test when the patient can no longer

22
00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:11.064
correctly indicate the direction of the ease or they complete the test.

23
00:01:11.064 --> 00:01:12.240
Now record the

24
00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:12.885
acuity achieved.

25
00:01:12.885 --> 00:01:17.400
The left side of the chart is labelled in Snellen notation with log mar

26
00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:18.347
on the right.

27
00:01:18.347 --> 00:01:23.400
Snellen is recorded as a fraction with a test distance on top and the last full

28
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:25.427
line seen correctly on the bottom.

29
00:01:25.427 --> 00:01:29.480
If some of the next line was also seen then right plus

30
00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:32.342
one or plus two for extra letters seen.

31
00:01:32.342 --> 00:01:35.920
For a log mar again record the last full line

32
00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:43.240
read correctly but this time subtract 0.07 if they managed one extra letter correctly

33
00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:45.897
or 0.14 if they saw two.

34
00:01:45.897 --> 00:01:49.440
If the patient cannot see even the biggest

35
00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:53.307
e at 3 metres then half the distance to 1.5 metres.

36
00:01:53.307 --> 00:01:56.120
If they still cannot see at this distance

37
00:01:56.120 --> 00:02:01.424
then test if they can count your fingers, follow your hand movements or if

38
00:02:01.424 --> 00:02:02.560
they can perceive

39
00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:05.800
light.

40
00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:09.360
Record what they could see and the distance.

41
00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.251
If the patient's vision is reduced, less than 3 over 3 or log mar

42
00:02:13.251 --> 00:02:14.640
0, then also test with

43
00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:16.200
a pinhole.

44
00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:20.160
This can show if the vision is reduced due to lack of glasses.

45
00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:22.867
You can make a set of pinholes by pushing a pin through the near

46
00:02:22.867 --> 00:02:23.640
card where the black

47
00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:24.640
dots are.

48
00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:28.362
Ask the patient to hold the card over the eye you are testing while

49
00:02:28.362 --> 00:02:29.520
covering their other eye with

50
00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:30.022
their palm.

51
00:02:30.022 --> 00:02:35.040
They will need to move it up or down a little so they can see through at least one hole.

52
00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:40.800
Repeat distance testing with the pinhole and record the results, making it clear that this test

53
00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:42.418
was with a pinhole.

54
00:02:42.418 --> 00:02:44.440
Now test near visual acuity.

55
00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:48.080
Ask the patient to hold the chart 40 centimeters

56
00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:50.025
away at a typical reading angle.

57
00:02:50.025 --> 00:02:52.943
If they own reading glasses test with them on.

58
00:02:52.943 --> 00:02:54.240
If not, then test

59
00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:54.591
unaided.

60
00:02:54.591 --> 00:02:57.400
Aspen to keep both eyes open this time.

61
00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:00.560
Explain that you want them to read out loud,

62
00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:03.769
starting with the biggest letters at the top of the chart.

63
00:03:03.769 --> 00:03:05.520
If the patient cannot read well,

64
00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:09.564
then aspen to point to small words or certain letters such as O, name

65
00:03:09.564 --> 00:03:10.720
letters in the words,

66
00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:14.951
match letters to a matching card or copy the shapes of letters in the

67
00:03:14.951 --> 00:03:16.000
ear with their finger.

68
00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:21.670
Stop the test when the patient can no longer read accurately and comfortably.

69
00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:22.720
Record the number

70
00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:25.131
of the last line they saw clearly.

71
00:03:25.131 --> 00:03:28.920
If their near vision is worse than N8, then repeat with

72
00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:31.000
reading glasses of different powers.
